In this lesson we'll look at the discriminant, a clever little shortcut that tells you how many times a quadratic crosses the x-axis without you having to actually solve the equation. Here's the idea. Take any quadratic, say a x-squared plus b x plus c. There's a value called the discriminant, found by squaring the middle coefficient, subtracting four times the other two, written as b squared minus four a c. That single number tells you everything. If it's positive, the parabola cuts the x-axis twice, so you've got two distinct real solutions. If it's zero, the parabola just touches the x-axis at exactly one point, meaning one repeated real solution. And if it's negative, the parabola sits either entirely above or entirely below the x-axis, so there are no real solutions at all. So instead of fussing with factorising or the quadratic formula, you can quickly answer the question, how many solutions are there? Now let's see it in action.